{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791286875,"nonce":"LiTS0SG2uv2cFpx+dn4f3LwKs5U=","title":"Python SOR: boxed grid dimensions keep _idx arithmetic dynamic after typed dispatch","message":"The tuple-key and override-dispatch work for c2558354203cdaa8387dac83f89ed28bce391ecbb10ccea550893773b6d3af7f and 054aec3bc2e926f8bf0cc763c2263b0b2f58af188d5bb72f4133cd732d7650ec now produces Array2D.__getitem__(Tuple(Int, Int))-\u003eFloat, __setitem__ with a typed key, and a class-guarded SOR_execute entry reached by the ordinary dynamic caller. Its generic body remains available for other receiver kinds.\n\nThe shipped scimark kernel still constructs its grid from n, cycles, Array = map(eval, args). The physical width and height fields remain Object. The real Array2D$_idx$s0 has x:Int, y:Int but returns Object: the bounds comparisons and y * self.width + x still use boxed fields and dynamic arithmetic. In a conformance fixture whose constructor dimensions are statically Int, the same tuple-key route gives _idx(Int, Int)-\u003eInt. This is the remaining part of the _idx pin requested in c255835, moved here so the completed dispatch work can close separately.\n\nOn the NUC (i5-1250P, x86_64), LLVM enabled, full SOR 100x100 for 5000 cycles, three alternating before/after processes gave kernel medians 34142.323 -\u003e 11882.412 ms and wall medians 34334.594 -\u003e 12079.144 ms. Both binaries pass the shipped checksum against CPython. A subsequent full --fast suite gives SOR 11940.474 ms against PyPy 205.662 ms (58.06x slower). This establishes the remaining gap, not the fraction caused by the dimensions; profile before attributing the whole gap to them.\n\nInvestigate general numeric-field constraints or guarded field specialization from uses/runtime feedback. Keep the actual Object storage layout and correct generic fallback; do not reinterpret boxed field words as native integers. Any cached unboxing must account for writes, calls that can mutate fields, subclasses, noninteger dimensions and bounds exceptions. Inspect the emitted HIR/Cranelift/LLVM and measure the normal tiered path, rather than forcing functions into LLVM.\n\nReproduce with an LLVM-enabled release zypy and:\n  python3 crates/zyntax_python/benchmarks/speed/run.py --fast --only scimark_sor --zypy \u003czypy\u003e --pypy \u003cpypy3\u003e --python \u003cpython3\u003e --out /tmp/sor.json\nTrace the shipped check with ZYNTAX_TRACE_TYPES=1 and the shipped scimark/util/optparse files staged beside it. Pin actual benchmark integer arithmetic when the field specialization is implemented, plus dynamic-field mutation/fallback correctness.","files":null}]}